{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R2KitbFU3GXXwBq9S4k8c2","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/source/tables.rst:15:    table.add_column(\"Released\", justify=\"right\", style=\"cyan\", no_wrap=True)\ndocs/source/tables.rst:144:- ``no_wrap`` Set to True to prevent this column from wrapping.\ndocs/source/pretty.rst:38:If you set the ``max_length`` argument to an integer, Rich will truncate containers with more than the given number of elements. If data is truncated, Rich will display an ellipsis ``...`` and the number of elements not shown.\ndocs/source/text.rst:48:- ``overflow`` should be \"fold\", \"crop\", or \"ellipsis\", and will override default overflow.\ndocs/source/text.rst:49:- ``no_wrap`` prevents wrapping if the text is longer then the available width.\ndocs/source/console.rst:173:You can specify how Rich should handle overflow with the ``overflow`` argument to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` which should be one of the following strings: \"fold\", \"crop\", \"ellipsis\", or \"ignore\". The default is \"fold\" which will put any excess characters on the following line, creating as many new lines as required to fit the text.\ndocs/source/console.rst:175:The \"crop\" method truncates the text at the end of the line, discarding any characters that would overflow.\ndocs/source/console.rst:177:The \"ellipsis\" method is similar to \"crop\", but will insert an ellipsis character (\"…\") at the end of any text that has been truncated.\ndocs/source/console.rst:179:The following code demonstrates the basic overflow methods::\ndocs/source/console.rst:187:    overflow_methods: List[OverflowMethod] = [\"fold\", \"crop\", \"ellipsis\"]\ndocs/source/console.rst:188:    for overflow in overflow_methods:\ndocs/source/console.rst:189:        console.rule(overflow)\ndocs/source/console.rst:190:        console.print(supercali, overflow=overflow, style=\"bold blue\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:205:    <span style=\"color: #00ff00\">── </span>ellipsis<span style=\"color: #00ff00\"> ──</span>\ndocs/source/console.rst:210:You can also set overflow to \"ignore\" which allows text to run on to the next line. In practice this will look the same as \"crop\" unless you also set ``crop=False`` when calling :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print`.\ndocs/source/live.rst:14:    If you see ellipsis \"...\", this indicates that the terminal is not tall enough to show the full table.\ndocs/source/live.rst:94:Vertical overflow\ndocs/source/live.rst:97:By default, the live display will display ellipsis if the renderable is too large for the terminal. You can adjust this by setting the\ndocs/source/live.rst:98:``vertical_overflow`` argument on the :class:`~rich.live.Live` constructor.\ndocs/source/live.rst:101:- \"ellipsis\" Similar to crop except last line of the terminal is replaced with \"...\". This is the default behavior."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ad2b736-86b8-4e70-a2e1-ef0b0aa98134","uuid":"284bdc0d-9d97-41f8-893d-fbaa920dfeec","timestamp":"2026-10-26T04:49:12.816Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"docs/source/tables.rst:15:    table.add_column(\"Released\", justify=\"right\", style=\"cyan\", no_wrap=True)\ndocs/source/tables.rst:144:- ``no_wrap`` Set to True to prevent this column from wrapping.\ndocs/source/pretty.rst:38:If you set the ``max_length`` argument to an integer, Rich will truncate containers with more than the given number of elements. If data is truncated, Rich will display an ellipsis ``...`` and the number of elements not shown.\ndocs/source/text.rst:48:- ``overflow`` should be \"fold\", \"crop\", or \"ellipsis\", and will override default overflow.\ndocs/source/text.rst:49:- ``no_wrap`` prevents wrapping if the text is longer then the available width.\ndocs/source/console.rst:173:You can specify how Rich should handle overflow with the ``overflow`` argument to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` which should be one of the following